{"subjectId":"dsa-basics","id":"queue","title":"Queue","group":"Stacks & Queues","order":15,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">A queue is a <strong>First In, First Out</strong> (FIFO) collection: elements enter at the <em>rear</em> and leave from the <em>front</em>, exactly like a line at a ticket counter. It is the data structure behind scheduling, buffering and breadth-first search.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Queue</strong> — a linear data structure where insertion (<code>enqueue</code>) happens at one end, the rear, and deletion (<code>dequeue</code>) happens at the other end, the front. The element that has waited longest is served first.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>A queue after enqueue(10), enqueue(15), enqueue(20), enqueue(30), enqueue(40) — 10 leaves first</figcaption><pre><code>dequeue()  ◀──  ┌────┬────┬────┬────┬────┐  ◀──  enqueue(x)\n                │ 10 │ 15 │ 20 │ 30 │ 40 │\n                └────┴────┴────┴────┴────┘\n                  ↑                   ↑\n                front               rear</code></pre></figure>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-list-check\"></i>Operations</h3>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Operation</th><th>What it does</th><th>Time</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code>enqueue(x)</code></td><td>Insert <code>x</code> at the rear</td><td>O(1)</td></tr><tr><td><code>dequeue()</code></td><td>Remove and return the front element</td><td>O(1)</td></tr><tr><td><code>front()</code> / <code>getFront()</code></td><td>Return the front element without removing it</td><td>O(1)</td></tr><tr><td><code>rear()</code> / <code>getRear()</code></td><td>Return the rear element without removing it</td><td>O(1)</td></tr><tr><td><code>isEmpty()</code></td><td><code>true</code> when the queue has no elements</td><td>O(1)</td></tr><tr><td><code>size()</code></td><td>Number of elements</td><td>O(1)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<p>Every operation is O(1) — <em>provided the implementation is right</em>. A plain array where <code>dequeue</code> removes index 0 is the classic way to get this wrong: every remaining element shifts one slot left, so <code>dequeue</code> becomes O(n). The circular-array section below fixes that.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p><strong>Underflow</strong> — <code>dequeue()</code> or <code>front()</code> on an empty queue. <strong>Overflow</strong> — <code>enqueue()</code> on a fixed-capacity queue that is full. Check <code>isEmpty()</code> / <code>isFull()</code> first; the built-in containers raise (Python/C++ UB/Java <code>remove</code>) or return <code>null</code>/<code>undefined</code> (Java <code>poll</code>, JavaScript <code>shift</code>).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-apps\"></i>Where queues are used</h3>\n<div class=\"grid grid--3\"><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-ticket\"></i>One resource, many consumers</h3><p>Ticket counters, printer spooling, a single CPU shared by many processes — whoever asked first is served first.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-device-desktop\"></i>Buffers between fast and slow devices</h3><p>The keyboard buffer, network sockets and audio/video pipelines queue data produced faster than it is consumed.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-cpu\"></i>Operating systems</h3><p>FCFS (first-come, first-served) scheduling, semaphore wait lists, I/O request queues, spooling.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-network\"></i>Networks and messaging</h3><p>Routers and switches queue packets; mail servers and message brokers (RabbitMQ, Kafka) queue jobs.</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-topology-star\"></i>Breadth-first search</h3><p>BFS on graphs and level-order traversal of trees visit nodes in the order they were discovered — a queue (see Trees and Graphs).</p></div><div class=\"card\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-shuffle\"></i>Variations</h3><p><strong>Deque</strong> (both ends), <strong>priority queue</strong> (served by priority, not arrival), <strong>double-ended priority queue</strong> (min and max).</p></div></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-box\"></i>Using the built-in containers</h3>\n<p>Every language ships a ready-made queue. The rule is the same everywhere: add at the back, remove at the front, and make sure removing at the front is O(1).</p>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from collections import deque\n\nq = deque()                       # O(1) at both ends\nq.append(10)                      # enqueue at the rear\nq.append(20)\nq.append(30)\nprint(\"front:\", q[0])\nprint(\"rear:\", q[-1])\nprint(\"dequeued:\", q.popleft())   # dequeue from the front\nprint(\"dequeued:\", q.popleft())\nprint(\"size:\", len(q))\nprint(\"empty:\", len(q) == 0)</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;queue&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nint main() {\n    queue&lt;int&gt; q;                 // std::queue adapts a std::deque\n    q.push(10);                   // enqueue at the back\n    q.push(20);\n    q.push(30);\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"front: \" &lt;&lt; q.front() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"rear: \" &lt;&lt; q.back() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"dequeued: \" &lt;&lt; q.front() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\"; q.pop();   // pop() returns nothing in C++\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"dequeued: \" &lt;&lt; q.front() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\"; q.pop();\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"size: \" &lt;&lt; q.size() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"empty: \" &lt;&lt; (q.empty() ? \"True\" : \"False\") &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.ArrayDeque;\n\npublic class Main {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        ArrayDeque&lt;Integer&gt; q = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();   // implements Queue (and Deque)\n        q.offer(10);                                  // enqueue at the tail\n        q.offer(20);\n        q.offer(30);\n        System.out.println(\"front: \" + q.peek());\n        System.out.println(\"rear: \" + q.peekLast());  // a Deque extra — Queue alone has no rear access\n        System.out.println(\"dequeued: \" + q.poll());  // dequeue from the head\n        System.out.println(\"dequeued: \" + q.poll());\n        System.out.println(\"size: \" + q.size());\n        System.out.println(\"empty: \" + (q.isEmpty() ? \"True\" : \"False\"));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>const q = [];                       // an array used as a queue\nq.push(10);                         // enqueue at the end\nq.push(20);\nq.push(30);\nconsole.log(\"front:\", q[0]);\nconsole.log(\"rear:\", q[q.length - 1]);\nconsole.log(\"dequeued:\", q.shift());   // O(n): every element moves one slot left\nconsole.log(\"dequeued:\", q.shift());\nconsole.log(\"size:\", q.length);\nconsole.log(\"empty:\", q.length === 0 ? \"True\" : \"False\");</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>front: 10\nrear: 30\ndequeued: 10\ndequeued: 20\nsize: 1\nempty: False</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"table-wrap\"><table><thead><tr><th>Language</th><th>Container</th><th>enqueue</th><th>dequeue</th><th>front</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Python</td><td><code>collections.deque</code></td><td><code>append(x)</code></td><td><code>popleft()</code></td><td><code>q[0]</code></td><td>O(1) at both ends; <code>q[-1]</code> is the rear</td></tr><tr><td>Python</td><td><code>queue.Queue</code></td><td><code>put(x)</code></td><td><code>get()</code></td><td>—</td><td>thread-safe, blocking — for producer/consumer code, not algorithms</td></tr><tr><td>C++</td><td><code>std::queue&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td><code>push(x)</code></td><td><code>pop()</code> (returns nothing)</td><td><code>front()</code></td><td><code>back()</code> is the rear; adapts <code>std::deque</code></td></tr><tr><td>Java</td><td><code>ArrayDeque&lt;T&gt;</code> / <code>LinkedList&lt;T&gt;</code> as <code>Queue&lt;T&gt;</code></td><td><code>offer(x)</code> / <code>add(x)</code></td><td><code>poll()</code> / <code>remove()</code></td><td><code>peek()</code> / <code>element()</code></td><td><code>offer/poll/peek</code> return <code>false</code>/<code>null</code> on failure, <code>add/remove/element</code> throw</td></tr><tr><td>JavaScript</td><td><code>Array</code></td><td><code>push(x)</code></td><td><code>shift()</code></td><td><code>q[0]</code></td><td><code>shift()</code> is O(n) — fine for small queues, a trap in BFS on large graphs</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>Using a Python <strong>list</strong> as a queue. <code>q.pop(0)</code> shifts every remaining element, so a loop that dequeues <code>n</code> items costs O(n²). The notes' original list version is shown below only to make the point — reach for <code>collections.deque</code>.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>q = []\nq.append(10)\nq.append(20)\nq.append(30)\nprint(q)          # [10, 20, 30]\nprint(q.pop(0))   # 10   ← removes the front, but shifts 20 and 30 left: O(n)\nprint(q)          # [20, 30]\nq.pop(0)\nprint(q)          # [30]</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><code>queue.Queue</code> is for threads: <code>get()</code> <strong>blocks</strong> until an item arrives, so calling it on an empty queue in single-threaded code hangs the program. Use <code>get_nowait()</code> / <code>put_nowait()</code> if you must use it outside a producer/consumer setting.</p></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from queue import Queue\n\nq = Queue()\nq.put(10)\nq.put(20)\nprint(q.get())            # 10\nprint(q.qsize())          # 1\nprint(q.empty())          # False\nprint(q.get_nowait())     # 20</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-link\"></i>Implementing a queue with a linked list</h3>\n<p>Keep two pointers: <code>front</code> (the head, where we dequeue) and <code>rear</code> (the tail, where we enqueue). Appending at the tail and removing at the head of a singly linked list are both O(1), and the queue never needs resizing.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>enqueue(10), enqueue(20), enqueue(30) — the oldest node is the head</figcaption><pre><code>front                                   rear\n ↓                                       ↓\n┌────┬───┐   ┌────┬───┐   ┌────┬───┐\n│ 10 │ ●─┼──▶│ 20 │ ●─┼──▶│ 30 │ ∅ │   enqueue(40): rear.next = node, rear = node\n└────┴───┘   └────┴───┘   └────┴───┘   dequeue():   front = front.next</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">enqueue(x)</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Create a node. If the queue is empty, <code>front = rear = node</code>; otherwise <code>rear.next = node</code> and <code>rear = node</code>.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">dequeue()</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Save <code>front.value</code>, move <code>front = front.next</code>. If <code>front</code> became <code>null</code>, also set <code>rear = null</code> — the classic bug is leaving <code>rear</code> pointing at the freed node.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">getFront / getRear</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Read <code>front.value</code> / <code>rear.value</code> — O(1) because we keep both pointers.</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class Node:\n    def __init__(self, value):\n        self.value = value\n        self.next = None\n\nclass Queue:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.front = None        # dequeue here (head)\n        self.rear = None         # enqueue here (tail)\n        self.size = 0\n\n    def enqueue(self, value):\n        node = Node(value)\n        if self.rear is None:            # empty: the node is both front and rear\n            self.front = self.rear = node\n        else:\n            self.rear.next = node        # link after the old tail\n            self.rear = node\n        self.size += 1\n\n    def dequeue(self):\n        if self.front is None:\n            raise IndexError(\"dequeue from empty queue\")\n        node = self.front\n        self.front = node.next\n        if self.front is None:           # became empty: rear must not dangle\n            self.rear = None\n        self.size -= 1\n        return node.value\n\n    def get_front(self):\n        return None if self.front is None else self.front.value\n\n    def get_rear(self):\n        return None if self.rear is None else self.rear.value\n\n    def is_empty(self):\n        return self.size == 0\n\nq = Queue()\nfor x in (10, 20, 30):\n    q.enqueue(x)\nprint(\"front:\", q.get_front())\nprint(\"rear:\", q.get_rear())\nprint(\"dequeued:\", q.dequeue())\nprint(\"front:\", q.get_front())\nprint(\"rear:\", q.get_rear())\nprint(\"size:\", q.size)\nprint(\"empty:\", q.is_empty())</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;stdexcept&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nstruct Node {\n    int value;\n    Node* next;\n    Node(int v) : value(v), next(nullptr) {}\n};\n\nclass Queue {\n    Node* front = nullptr;     // dequeue here (head)\n    Node* rear = nullptr;      // enqueue here (tail)\n    int count = 0;\npublic:\n    ~Queue() { while (front) dequeue(); }\n    void enqueue(int v) {\n        Node* node = new Node(v);\n        if (!rear) front = rear = node;          // empty: the node is both front and rear\n        else { rear-&gt;next = node; rear = node; } // link after the old tail\n        count++;\n    }\n    int dequeue() {\n        if (!front) throw runtime_error(\"dequeue from empty queue\");\n        Node* node = front;\n        int v = node-&gt;value;\n        front = node-&gt;next;\n        if (!front) rear = nullptr;              // became empty: rear must not dangle\n        delete node;\n        count--;\n        return v;\n    }\n    int getFront() const { return front-&gt;value; }\n    int getRear() const { return rear-&gt;value; }\n    bool isEmpty() const { return count == 0; }\n    int size() const { return count; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Queue q;\n    for (int x : {10, 20, 30}) q.enqueue(x);\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"front: \" &lt;&lt; q.getFront() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"rear: \" &lt;&lt; q.getRear() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"dequeued: \" &lt;&lt; q.dequeue() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"front: \" &lt;&lt; q.getFront() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"rear: \" &lt;&lt; q.getRear() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"size: \" &lt;&lt; q.size() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"empty: \" &lt;&lt; (q.isEmpty() ? \"True\" : \"False\") &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static class Node {\n        int value; Node next;\n        Node(int v) { value = v; }\n    }\n\n    static class Queue {\n        Node front, rear;          // dequeue at front (head), enqueue at rear (tail)\n        int size;\n        void enqueue(int v) {\n            Node node = new Node(v);\n            if (rear == null) front = rear = node;      // empty: the node is both front and rear\n            else { rear.next = node; rear = node; }     // link after the old tail\n            size++;\n        }\n        int dequeue() {\n            if (front == null) throw new RuntimeException(\"dequeue from empty queue\");\n            int v = front.value;\n            front = front.next;\n            if (front == null) rear = null;             // became empty: rear must not dangle\n            size--;\n            return v;\n        }\n        Integer getFront() { return front == null ? null : front.value; }\n        Integer getRear() { return rear == null ? null : rear.value; }\n        boolean isEmpty() { return size == 0; }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Queue q = new Queue();\n        for (int x : new int[]{10, 20, 30}) q.enqueue(x);\n        System.out.println(\"front: \" + q.getFront());\n        System.out.println(\"rear: \" + q.getRear());\n        System.out.println(\"dequeued: \" + q.dequeue());\n        System.out.println(\"front: \" + q.getFront());\n        System.out.println(\"rear: \" + q.getRear());\n        System.out.println(\"size: \" + q.size);\n        System.out.println(\"empty: \" + (q.isEmpty() ? \"True\" : \"False\"));\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Node {\n    constructor(value) { this.value = value; this.next = null; }\n}\n\nclass Queue {\n    constructor() { this.front = null; this.rear = null; this.size = 0; }\n    enqueue(value) {\n        const node = new Node(value);\n        if (this.rear === null) this.front = this.rear = node;   // empty: both ends\n        else { this.rear.next = node; this.rear = node; }        // link after the old tail\n        this.size++;\n    }\n    dequeue() {\n        if (this.front === null) throw new Error(\"dequeue from empty queue\");\n        const node = this.front;\n        this.front = node.next;\n        if (this.front === null) this.rear = null;               // became empty: rear must not dangle\n        this.size--;\n        return node.value;\n    }\n    getFront() { return this.front ? this.front.value : null; }\n    getRear() { return this.rear ? this.rear.value : null; }\n    isEmpty() { return this.size === 0; }\n}\n\nconst q = new Queue();\nfor (const x of [10, 20, 30]) q.enqueue(x);\nconsole.log(\"front:\", q.getFront());\nconsole.log(\"rear:\", q.getRear());\nconsole.log(\"dequeued:\", q.dequeue());\nconsole.log(\"front:\", q.getFront());\nconsole.log(\"rear:\", q.getRear());\nconsole.log(\"size:\", q.size);\nconsole.log(\"empty:\", q.isEmpty() ? \"True\" : \"False\");</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>front: 10\nrear: 30\ndequeued: 10\nfront: 20\nrear: 30\nsize: 2\nempty: False</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(1) per operation</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n) for n elements</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>Enqueueing at the <strong>head</strong> and dequeueing at the tail would need the node <em>before</em> the tail, which a singly linked list cannot reach in O(1). Always enqueue at the tail and dequeue at the head.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-repeat\"></i>Implementing a queue with a circular array</h3>\n<p>A fixed array of capacity <code>cap</code> has two naive designs, and both are bad. If <code>dequeue</code> removes <code>slots[0]</code> and shifts everything, it is O(n). If instead it just advances a <code>front</code> index, each dequeue leaves a dead slot behind: after a few dequeues <code>rear</code> reaches the end and <code>enqueue</code> fails even though the array is half empty.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Advancing front without wrapping wastes every slot that has been dequeued</figcaption><pre><code>Plain array, dequeue by advancing front (capacity 5):\n\n  index:   0     1     2     3     4\n        ┌─────┬─────┬─────┬─────┬─────┐\n        │  ×  │  ×  │ 30  │ 40  │ 50  │   front = 2, rear = 5\n        └─────┴─────┴─────┴─────┴─────┘\n           ↑ dead slots              ↑ rear is past the end:\n                                       enqueue(60) fails although two slots are free</code></pre></figure>\n<p>The fix is to let both indices <strong>wrap around</strong> with the modulo operator: <code>rear = (rear + 1) % cap</code> and <code>front = (front + 1) % cap</code>. The array is treated as a ring, so a dequeued slot is reused by the next enqueue.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>The ring reuses the slots freed by dequeue; the logical order is read from front, wrapping with % cap</figcaption><pre><code>Circular array — same state, then enqueue(60), enqueue(70):\n\n  index:   0     1     2     3     4\n        ┌─────┬─────┬─────┬─────┬─────┐\n        │ 60  │ 70  │ 30  │ 40  │ 50  │   rear wrapped: 5 % 5 = 0, then 1\n        └─────┴─────┴─────┴─────┴─────┘\n                       ↑ front = 2\n           ↑ rear = 2 (next free slot) — equal to front, so size tells us: full\n\n  logical order (front → rear):  30  40  50  60  70</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>When <code>front == rear</code> the ring is either <strong>empty or full</strong> — the indices alone cannot tell. Either keep a <code>size</code> counter (done below) or deliberately leave one slot unused and treat <code>(rear + 1) % cap == front</code> as full.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>capacity = 5; enqueue 10 20 30 40 50; dequeue ×2; enqueue 60 70</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>slots: 60 70 30 40 50 — logical order 30 40 50 60 70</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class CircularQueue:\n    def __init__(self, capacity):\n        self.slots = [None] * capacity\n        self.cap = capacity\n        self.front = 0          # index of the first element\n        self.rear = 0           # index where the next element goes\n        self.size = 0           # needed: front == rear for both empty and full\n\n    def is_empty(self):\n        return self.size == 0\n\n    def is_full(self):\n        return self.size == self.cap\n\n    def enqueue(self, value):\n        if self.is_full():\n            raise OverflowError(\"queue is full\")\n        slot = self.rear\n        self.slots[slot] = value\n        self.rear = (self.rear + 1) % self.cap     # wrap around\n        self.size += 1\n        return slot\n\n    def dequeue(self):\n        if self.is_empty():\n            raise IndexError(\"queue is empty\")\n        value = self.slots[self.front]\n        self.front = (self.front + 1) % self.cap   # wrap around\n        self.size -= 1\n        return value\n\n    def get_front(self):\n        return self.slots[self.front]\n\n    def get_rear(self):\n        return self.slots[(self.rear - 1 + self.cap) % self.cap]\n\nq = CircularQueue(5)\nfor x in (10, 20, 30, 40, 50):\n    q.enqueue(x)\nprint(\"front:\", q.get_front())\nprint(\"rear:\", q.get_rear())\nprint(\"full:\", q.is_full())\nprint(\"dequeued:\", q.dequeue())\nprint(\"dequeued:\", q.dequeue())\nprint(\"enqueue 60 → slot\", q.enqueue(60))\nprint(\"enqueue 70 → slot\", q.enqueue(70))\nprint(\"slots:\", *q.slots)\nprint(\"front =\", q.front, \"rear =\", q.rear, \"size =\", q.size)\nprint(\"in order:\", *(q.slots[(q.front + i) % q.cap] for i in range(q.size)))</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;stdexcept&gt;\n#include &lt;vector&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nclass CircularQueue {\npublic:\n    vector&lt;int&gt; slots;\n    int cap, front = 0, rear = 0, size = 0;   // size: front == rear for both empty and full\n    CircularQueue(int capacity) : slots(capacity, 0), cap(capacity) {}\n    bool isEmpty() const { return size == 0; }\n    bool isFull() const { return size == cap; }\n    int enqueue(int value) {\n        if (isFull()) throw runtime_error(\"queue is full\");\n        int slot = rear;\n        slots[slot] = value;\n        rear = (rear + 1) % cap;              // wrap around\n        size++;\n        return slot;\n    }\n    int dequeue() {\n        if (isEmpty()) throw runtime_error(\"queue is empty\");\n        int value = slots[front];\n        front = (front + 1) % cap;            // wrap around\n        size--;\n        return value;\n    }\n    int getFront() const { return slots[front]; }\n    int getRear() const { return slots[(rear - 1 + cap) % cap]; }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    CircularQueue q(5);\n    for (int x : {10, 20, 30, 40, 50}) q.enqueue(x);\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"front: \" &lt;&lt; q.getFront() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"rear: \" &lt;&lt; q.getRear() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"full: \" &lt;&lt; (q.isFull() ? \"True\" : \"False\") &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"dequeued: \" &lt;&lt; q.dequeue() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"dequeued: \" &lt;&lt; q.dequeue() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"enqueue 60 → slot \" &lt;&lt; q.enqueue(60) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"enqueue 70 → slot \" &lt;&lt; q.enqueue(70) &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"slots:\";\n    for (int v : q.slots) cout &lt;&lt; \" \" &lt;&lt; v;\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"front = \" &lt;&lt; q.front &lt;&lt; \" rear = \" &lt;&lt; q.rear &lt;&lt; \" size = \" &lt;&lt; q.size &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"in order:\";\n    for (int i = 0; i &lt; q.size; i++) cout &lt;&lt; \" \" &lt;&lt; q.slots[(q.front + i) % q.cap];\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>public class Main {\n    static class CircularQueue {\n        int[] slots;\n        int cap, front = 0, rear = 0, size = 0;   // size: front == rear for both empty and full\n        CircularQueue(int capacity) { slots = new int[capacity]; cap = capacity; }\n        boolean isEmpty() { return size == 0; }\n        boolean isFull() { return size == cap; }\n        int enqueue(int value) {\n            if (isFull()) throw new RuntimeException(\"queue is full\");\n            int slot = rear;\n            slots[slot] = value;\n            rear = (rear + 1) % cap;              // wrap around\n            size++;\n            return slot;\n        }\n        int dequeue() {\n            if (isEmpty()) throw new RuntimeException(\"queue is empty\");\n            int value = slots[front];\n            front = (front + 1) % cap;            // wrap around\n            size--;\n            return value;\n        }\n        int getFront() { return slots[front]; }\n        int getRear() { return slots[(rear - 1 + cap) % cap]; }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        CircularQueue q = new CircularQueue(5);\n        for (int x : new int[]{10, 20, 30, 40, 50}) q.enqueue(x);\n        System.out.println(\"front: \" + q.getFront());\n        System.out.println(\"rear: \" + q.getRear());\n        System.out.println(\"full: \" + (q.isFull() ? \"True\" : \"False\"));\n        System.out.println(\"dequeued: \" + q.dequeue());\n        System.out.println(\"dequeued: \" + q.dequeue());\n        System.out.println(\"enqueue 60 → slot \" + q.enqueue(60));\n        System.out.println(\"enqueue 70 → slot \" + q.enqueue(70));\n        StringBuilder sb = new StringBuilder(\"slots:\");\n        for (int v : q.slots) sb.append(' ').append(v);\n        System.out.println(sb);\n        System.out.println(\"front = \" + q.front + \" rear = \" + q.rear + \" size = \" + q.size);\n        sb = new StringBuilder(\"in order:\");\n        for (int i = 0; i &lt; q.size; i++) sb.append(' ').append(q.slots[(q.front + i) % q.cap]);\n        System.out.println(sb);\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class CircularQueue {\n    constructor(capacity) {\n        this.slots = new Array(capacity).fill(null);\n        this.cap = capacity;\n        this.front = 0;        // index of the first element\n        this.rear = 0;         // index where the next element goes\n        this.size = 0;         // needed: front == rear for both empty and full\n    }\n    isEmpty() { return this.size === 0; }\n    isFull() { return this.size === this.cap; }\n    enqueue(value) {\n        if (this.isFull()) throw new Error(\"queue is full\");\n        const slot = this.rear;\n        this.slots[slot] = value;\n        this.rear = (this.rear + 1) % this.cap;      // wrap around\n        this.size++;\n        return slot;\n    }\n    dequeue() {\n        if (this.isEmpty()) throw new Error(\"queue is empty\");\n        const value = this.slots[this.front];\n        this.front = (this.front + 1) % this.cap;    // wrap around\n        this.size--;\n        return value;\n    }\n    getFront() { return this.slots[this.front]; }\n    getRear() { return this.slots[(this.rear - 1 + this.cap) % this.cap]; }\n}\n\nconst q = new CircularQueue(5);\nfor (const x of [10, 20, 30, 40, 50]) q.enqueue(x);\nconsole.log(\"front:\", q.getFront());\nconsole.log(\"rear:\", q.getRear());\nconsole.log(\"full:\", q.isFull() ? \"True\" : \"False\");\nconsole.log(\"dequeued:\", q.dequeue());\nconsole.log(\"dequeued:\", q.dequeue());\nconsole.log(\"enqueue 60 → slot\", q.enqueue(60));\nconsole.log(\"enqueue 70 → slot\", q.enqueue(70));\nconsole.log(\"slots:\", q.slots.join(\" \"));\nconsole.log(\"front =\", q.front, \"rear =\", q.rear, \"size =\", q.size);\nconst order = [];\nfor (let i = 0; i &lt; q.size; i++) order.push(q.slots[(q.front + i) % q.cap]);\nconsole.log(\"in order:\", order.join(\" \"));</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>front: 10\nrear: 50\nfull: True\ndequeued: 10\ndequeued: 20\nenqueue 60 → slot 0\nenqueue 70 → slot 1\nslots: 60 70 30 40 50\nfront = 2 rear = 2 size = 5\nin order: 30 40 50 60 70</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time O(1) per operation</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(cap)</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p><code>getRear()</code> is <strong>not</strong> <code>slots[rear - 1]</code>: after a wrap <code>rear</code> can be 0, and index <code>-1</code> is out of bounds in C++/Java (and silently the last element in Python). Use <code>(rear - 1 + cap) % cap</code>. Also note that a dequeued slot is not cleared — printing the raw array shows stale values until they are overwritten.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Dynamic-array queues (<code>std::deque</code>, Java <code>ArrayDeque</code>, Python <code>deque</code>) are circular buffers that double the capacity when full, so they never overflow: amortised O(1) enqueue.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-stack-2\"></i>Problem: implement a stack using queues</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>push(10), push(20), push(30), top(), pop(), top()</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>30, 30, 20</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>A queue gives you the <em>oldest</em> element; a stack needs the <em>newest</em>. Make <code>push</code> expensive: put the new element into an empty auxiliary queue, move everything else behind it, then swap the queues. The front of <code>q1</code> is now always the most recently pushed element, so <code>pop</code> and <code>top</code> are plain O(1) queue operations.</p></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">push(x) — version 1</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Move everything from <code>q1</code> to <code>q2</code>, enqueue <code>x</code> into <code>q1</code>, move everything back from <code>q2</code> to <code>q1</code>. Two full copies per push.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">push(x) — optimised</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Enqueue <code>x</code> into <code>q2</code>, move everything from <code>q1</code> behind it, then <strong>swap</strong> <code>q1</code> and <code>q2</code>. One copy per push, same effect.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">pop() / top()</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Dequeue / peek the front of <code>q1</code> — O(1).</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">4</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">size() / isEmpty()</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Delegate to <code>q1</code>; <code>isFull()</code> is always <code>false</code> for a dynamic queue.</div></div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>The optimised push: the new element goes in first, older elements queue behind it</figcaption><pre><code>push(10):  q2 = [10]                 swap → q1 = [10]        q2 = []\npush(20):  q2 = [20] ← move 10 →     q2 = [20 10]   swap → q1 = [20 10]\npush(30):  q2 = [30] ← move 20 10 →  q2 = [30 20 10] swap → q1 = [30 20 10]\n                                                              ↑ front = top of stack</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>from collections import deque\n\nclass Stack:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.q1 = deque()      # holds the items, newest at the front\n        self.q2 = deque()      # auxiliary, empty between operations\n\n    def push(self, x):\n        self.q2.append(x)                      # a) x goes in first ...\n        while self.q1:                         # b) ... then everything older behind it\n            self.q2.append(self.q1.popleft())\n        self.q1, self.q2 = self.q2, self.q1    # c) swap: q1 holds the items again\n\n    def pop(self):\n        return self.q1.popleft()               # front of q1 is the newest item\n\n    def top(self):\n        return self.q1[0]\n\n    def size(self):\n        return len(self.q1)\n\n    def is_empty(self):\n        return len(self.q1) == 0\n\ns = Stack()\ns.push(10)\ns.push(20)\ns.push(30)\nprint(\"top:\", s.top())\nprint(\"popped:\", s.pop())\nprint(\"top:\", s.top())\nprint(\"size:\", s.size())</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;queue&gt;\n#include &lt;utility&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nclass Stack {\n    queue&lt;int&gt; q1, q2;         // q1 holds the items, newest at the front\npublic:\n    void push(int x) {\n        q2.push(x);                                            // a) x goes in first ...\n        while (!q1.empty()) { q2.push(q1.front()); q1.pop(); } // b) ... older items behind it\n        swap(q1, q2);                                          // c) q1 holds the items again\n    }\n    int pop() { int x = q1.front(); q1.pop(); return x; }     // front of q1 is the newest item\n    int top() const { return q1.front(); }\n    int size() const { return (int)q1.size(); }\n    bool isEmpty() const { return q1.empty(); }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Stack s;\n    s.push(10);\n    s.push(20);\n    s.push(30);\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"top: \" &lt;&lt; s.top() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"popped: \" &lt;&lt; s.pop() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"top: \" &lt;&lt; s.top() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"size: \" &lt;&lt; s.size() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.ArrayDeque;\nimport java.util.Queue;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static class Stack {\n        Queue&lt;Integer&gt; q1 = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();   // holds the items, newest at the front\n        Queue&lt;Integer&gt; q2 = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();   // auxiliary\n        void push(int x) {\n            q2.offer(x);                                   // a) x goes in first ...\n            while (!q1.isEmpty()) q2.offer(q1.poll());     // b) ... older items behind it\n            Queue&lt;Integer&gt; tmp = q1; q1 = q2; q2 = tmp;    // c) swap\n        }\n        int pop() { return q1.poll(); }                    // front of q1 is the newest item\n        int top() { return q1.peek(); }\n        int size() { return q1.size(); }\n        boolean isEmpty() { return q1.isEmpty(); }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Stack s = new Stack();\n        s.push(10);\n        s.push(20);\n        s.push(30);\n        System.out.println(\"top: \" + s.top());\n        System.out.println(\"popped: \" + s.pop());\n        System.out.println(\"top: \" + s.top());\n        System.out.println(\"size: \" + s.size());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Stack {\n    constructor() {\n        this.q1 = [];          // holds the items, newest at the front\n        this.q2 = [];          // auxiliary\n    }\n    push(x) {\n        this.q2.push(x);                                   // a) x goes in first ...\n        while (this.q1.length) this.q2.push(this.q1.shift()); // b) ... older items behind it\n        [this.q1, this.q2] = [this.q2, this.q1];           // c) swap\n    }\n    pop() { return this.q1.shift(); }                      // front of q1 is the newest item\n    top() { return this.q1[0]; }\n    size() { return this.q1.length; }\n    isEmpty() { return this.q1.length === 0; }\n}\n\nconst s = new Stack();\ns.push(10);\ns.push(20);\ns.push(30);\nconsole.log(\"top:\", s.top());\nconsole.log(\"popped:\", s.pop());\nconsole.log(\"top:\", s.top());\nconsole.log(\"size:\", s.size());</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>top: 30\npopped: 30\ntop: 20\nsize: 2</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time push O(n), pop/top O(1)</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n)</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p><strong>One queue is enough</strong>: enqueue <code>x</code>, then dequeue-and-re-enqueue the other <code>size − 1</code> elements so <code>x</code> rotates to the front. Same O(n) push, no auxiliary queue. The mirror design — cheap push, O(n) pop that rotates all but the last element — is also valid; pick the one that matches which operation is more frequent.</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-arrows-shuffle\"></i>Problem: implement a queue using two stacks</h3>\n<div class=\"callout callout--example\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Example</div><p><strong>Input:</strong> <code>enqueue(10), enqueue(20), enqueue(30), dequeue(), dequeue(), enqueue(40), front()</code><br><strong>Output:</strong> <code>10, 20, 30</code></p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Idea</div><p>Two LIFO reversals make a FIFO. Push every new element onto <code>inbox</code>. To dequeue, pop from <code>outbox</code>; when <code>outbox</code> is empty, pour all of <code>inbox</code> into it first — popping and pushing reverses the order, so the oldest element ends up on top of <code>outbox</code>.</p></div>\n<div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">1</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">enqueue(x)</div><div class=\"step-desc\"><code>inbox.push(x)</code> — O(1).</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">2</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">dequeue() / front()</div><div class=\"step-desc\">If <code>outbox</code> is empty, move every element from <code>inbox</code> to <code>outbox</code> (pop one, push the other). Then pop / peek <code>outbox</code>.</div></div></div><div class=\"step\"><div class=\"step-num\">3</div><div class=\"step-body\"><div class=\"step-title\">Why not pour every time?</div><div class=\"step-desc\">Only pour when <code>outbox</code> is empty. Elements already in <code>outbox</code> are older than anything in <code>inbox</code>, so the order stays correct, and each element is moved at most once.</div></div></div>\n<div class=\"tabs\"><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Python\"><figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>class Queue:\n    def __init__(self):\n        self.inbox = []        # enqueue pushes here\n        self.outbox = []       # dequeue pops from here (reversed = FIFO order)\n\n    def enqueue(self, x):\n        self.inbox.append(x)\n\n    def _pour(self):\n        if not self.outbox:                    # only when outbox is empty\n            while self.inbox:\n                self.outbox.append(self.inbox.pop())\n\n    def dequeue(self):\n        self._pour()\n        return self.outbox.pop()\n\n    def front(self):\n        self._pour()\n        return self.outbox[-1]\n\n    def size(self):\n        return len(self.inbox) + len(self.outbox)\n\nq = Queue()\nfor x in (10, 20, 30):\n    q.enqueue(x)\nprint(\"front:\", q.front())\nprint(\"dequeued:\", q.dequeue())\nprint(\"dequeued:\", q.dequeue())\nq.enqueue(40)\nprint(\"front:\", q.front())\nprint(\"size:\", q.size())</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"C++\"><figure data-lang=\"cpp\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>C++</figcaption><pre><code>#include &lt;iostream&gt;\n#include &lt;stack&gt;\nusing namespace std;\n\nclass Queue {\n    stack&lt;int&gt; inbox, outbox;  // enqueue pushes to inbox; dequeue pops from outbox\n    void pour() {\n        if (outbox.empty())                              // only when outbox is empty\n            while (!inbox.empty()) { outbox.push(inbox.top()); inbox.pop(); }\n    }\npublic:\n    void enqueue(int x) { inbox.push(x); }\n    int dequeue() { pour(); int x = outbox.top(); outbox.pop(); return x; }\n    int front() { pour(); return outbox.top(); }\n    int size() const { return (int)(inbox.size() + outbox.size()); }\n};\n\nint main() {\n    Queue q;\n    for (int x : {10, 20, 30}) q.enqueue(x);\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"front: \" &lt;&lt; q.front() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"dequeued: \" &lt;&lt; q.dequeue() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"dequeued: \" &lt;&lt; q.dequeue() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    q.enqueue(40);\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"front: \" &lt;&lt; q.front() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n    cout &lt;&lt; \"size: \" &lt;&lt; q.size() &lt;&lt; \"\\n\";\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"Java\"><figure data-lang=\"java\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Java</figcaption><pre><code>import java.util.ArrayDeque;\nimport java.util.Deque;\n\npublic class Main {\n    static class Queue {\n        Deque&lt;Integer&gt; inbox = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();    // enqueue pushes here\n        Deque&lt;Integer&gt; outbox = new ArrayDeque&lt;&gt;();   // dequeue pops from here\n        void enqueue(int x) { inbox.push(x); }\n        void pour() {\n            if (outbox.isEmpty())                     // only when outbox is empty\n                while (!inbox.isEmpty()) outbox.push(inbox.pop());\n        }\n        int dequeue() { pour(); return outbox.pop(); }\n        int front() { pour(); return outbox.peek(); }\n        int size() { return inbox.size() + outbox.size(); }\n    }\n\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        Queue q = new Queue();\n        for (int x : new int[]{10, 20, 30}) q.enqueue(x);\n        System.out.println(\"front: \" + q.front());\n        System.out.println(\"dequeued: \" + q.dequeue());\n        System.out.println(\"dequeued: \" + q.dequeue());\n        q.enqueue(40);\n        System.out.println(\"front: \" + q.front());\n        System.out.println(\"size: \" + q.size());\n    }\n}</code></pre></figure></div><div class=\"tab-panel\" data-label=\"JavaScript\"><figure data-lang=\"js\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>JavaScript</figcaption><pre><code>class Queue {\n    constructor() {\n        this.inbox = [];       // enqueue pushes here\n        this.outbox = [];      // dequeue pops from here (reversed = FIFO order)\n    }\n    enqueue(x) { this.inbox.push(x); }\n    pour() {\n        if (this.outbox.length === 0)                  // only when outbox is empty\n            while (this.inbox.length) this.outbox.push(this.inbox.pop());\n    }\n    dequeue() { this.pour(); return this.outbox.pop(); }\n    front() { this.pour(); return this.outbox[this.outbox.length - 1]; }\n    size() { return this.inbox.length + this.outbox.length; }\n}\n\nconst q = new Queue();\nfor (const x of [10, 20, 30]) q.enqueue(x);\nconsole.log(\"front:\", q.front());\nconsole.log(\"dequeued:\", q.dequeue());\nconsole.log(\"dequeued:\", q.dequeue());\nq.enqueue(40);\nconsole.log(\"front:\", q.front());\nconsole.log(\"size:\", q.size());</code></pre></figure></div></div><figure data-lang=\"text\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Output</figcaption><pre><code>front: 10\ndequeued: 10\ndequeued: 20\nfront: 30\nsize: 2</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"chips\"><span class=\"chip\">Time amortised O(1) per operation</span><span class=\"chip\">Space O(n)</span></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Pitfall</div><p>Pouring <code>inbox</code> into <code>outbox</code> while <code>outbox</code> still has elements breaks the order: the poured (newer) elements would land on top of older ones. Pour <strong>only when <code>outbox</code> is empty</strong>. Each element is pushed and popped at most twice, which is why the amortised cost is O(1) even though one dequeue can be O(n).</p></div>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-message-question\"></i>Interview Q&amp;A</h3>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Stack vs queue — when do you use which?</summary><div><p>A stack (LIFO) when the most recent thing matters: function calls, undo, bracket matching, DFS. A queue (FIFO) when arrival order matters: scheduling, buffering, BFS / level-order traversal. Both have O(1) insert and remove; the only difference is which end you remove from.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why is a circular array better than a plain array for a queue?</summary><div><p>A plain array either shifts all elements on every dequeue (O(n)) or wastes every slot it has dequeued, failing with a <em>full</em> error while most of the array is empty. Wrapping <code>front</code> and <code>rear</code> with <code>% capacity</code> lets freed slots be reused, so every operation is O(1) and all <code>capacity</code> slots are usable.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>In a circular queue front == rear — is it empty or full?</summary><div><p>Either, so the indices alone are ambiguous. Two standard fixes: keep a <code>size</code> counter (empty when <code>size == 0</code>, full when <code>size == capacity</code>), or sacrifice one slot and declare the queue full when <code>(rear + 1) % capacity == front</code>.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why not use a Python list or a JavaScript array as a queue?</summary><div><p><code>list.pop(0)</code> and <code>Array.shift()</code> remove the first element by shifting every other element left — O(n) per dequeue, O(n²) for a loop that drains the queue. Use <code>collections.deque</code> (O(1) <code>popleft</code>) in Python; in JavaScript write a small linked-list or two-pointer queue (or keep a <code>head</code> index into the array) for anything large, e.g. BFS over a big graph.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>How do you implement a queue with two stacks, and what is the complexity?</summary><div><p>Push into <code>inbox</code>. To dequeue, if <code>outbox</code> is empty pour everything from <code>inbox</code> into <code>outbox</code> (which reverses it), then pop <code>outbox</code>. A single dequeue can cost O(n), but each element is moved at most once, so <code>n</code> operations cost O(n) in total — amortised O(1).</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>What is a priority queue, and how does it differ from a queue?</summary><div><p>A queue serves elements in arrival order; a priority queue serves the element with the highest (or lowest) priority first, regardless of arrival. It is normally built on a binary heap: O(log n) insert and remove-top, O(1) peek. A deque is a different variation — a queue that allows insertion and removal at <em>both</em> ends.</p></div></details></section>"}